{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And if it be an unclean beast, then he shall redeem it according to your valuation.</b> This verse is not spoken about the firstborn, for one cannot say of the firstborn of an unclean beast, \"then he shall redeem it according to your valuation,\" and a donkey is not this, for the redemption of the firstborn of a donkey is only with a lamb, and it is a gift to the priest and is not consecrated property. Rather, the verse is spoken about consecrated property, for above it spoke of redeeming a clean animal that had a blemish, and here it speaks of one who consecrates an unclean animal for the maintenance of the House — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 27:27). And it is possible to resolve it thus: since it says, \"Only the firstborn that is firstborn to YHWH among beasts, no man shall consecrate it,\" it goes back and says, \"And if it be an unclean beast\" — this is the firstborn that he consecrated — \"then he shall redeem it according to your valuation,\" to explain that the law of the firstborn does not apply to an unclean animal except for the firstborn of a donkey, whose law has already been explained (Exodus 13:13). And it teaches that even if he consecrated it, it is not holy with the sanctity of a firstborn, but is like any other consecrated property and is redeemed. Therefore it says that the one who consecrated it shall redeem it with the addition of a fifth, and afterward he may acquire it according to your valuation. And the verse above, \"And if it be any unclean beast, of which they do not offer from it\" (Leviticus 27:11), according to its plain sense speaks of an unclean animal. And according to the midrash of our Sages (Temurah 32a) the verse is superfluous, because it repeated \"unclean, of which they do not offer from it a sacrifice to YHWH,\" therefore they expound: \"And if it be any unclean beast\" and \"of which they do not offer from it a sacrifice to YHWH\" — to include animals with permanent blemishes from which they do not offer a sacrifice to YHWH."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The reading 'הוא הבכור שהקדיש' assumes the subject is specifically the firstborn of a donkey that one attempted to consecrate; the alternative reading in Rashi's name treats the verse as unrelated to firstborn entirely."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:30.462651313Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}